      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nThe product roadmap is one of the most universal artifacts in business—and one of the most broken. Created to align teams around a shared direction, roadmaps more often produce the opposite: missed dates, demoralized engineers, and stakeholders who feel misled. The failure isn't in execution. It's baked into how roadmaps are conceived.\n\nFirst, most roadmaps traffic in false precision. A document that promises specific features on specific dates, twelve months out, pretends to knowledge nobody has. Markets shift, competitors move, technical surprises emerge, and—most importantly—teams learn things about their customers that invalidate earlier assumptions. Estimation error compounds with distance: a feature slotted for Q3 of next year carries months of embedded uncertainty. Yet the calendar format demands a date, and the date quietly becomes a commitment.\n\nSecond, roadmaps are typically organized around outputs rather than outcomes. They list what will be built, not which problems will be solved or which results achieved. Product thinker Melissa Perri calls this the \"build trap\": organizations that measure themselves by shipping features rather than creating value. A feature-based roadmap can be executed flawlessly and still fail, because nobody validated whether those features were worth building.\n\nThird, roadmaps are political documents. They are assembled through negotiation—with executives championing pet ideas, sales teams promising features to close deals, and the loudest customers drowning out the quiet majority. The result is less a strategy than a treaty: a record of who won which argument. Such roadmaps reflect internal power dynamics, not customer need.\n\nFourth, and most perniciously, roadmaps punish learning. Once published, a roadmap creates an audience. Changing it feels like breaking a promise; admitting a planned feature isn't worth building looks like failure. So teams ship things they no longer believe in to protect the document's credibility. The roadmap, designed to guide decisions, starts making them—badly. In a field where discovery is the entire point, the roadmap becomes a mechanism for ignoring what you discover.\n\nThe organizations that escape this pattern share a few traits. They plan in horizons—\"now, next, later\"—with detail decreasing as distance increases, making uncertainty explicit instead of hiding it behind false dates. They commit to outcomes (reduce churn, shorten onboarding) rather than features, preserving the freedom to find the best solution. And they treat the roadmap as a living hypothesis to be revised, not a contract to be honored.\n\nA roadmap should be a compass, not a train schedule. The moment it hardens into a list of promises, it stops describing the future and starts preventing it. Most roadmaps fail not because teams can't execute them, but because the document itself encodes certainty where none exists—and then penalizes the learning that might have led somewhere better.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nNearly every product organization produces a roadmap, yet remarkably few products follow one to success. The failure is rarely a matter of poor planning discipline. It is structural: the conventional roadmap rests on assumptions that guarantee its own obsolescence.\n\nThe first and deepest flaw is that roadmaps describe outputs when products succeed on outcomes. A typical roadmap is a list of features attached to dates. But features are solutions, and solutions are hypotheses about what customers need. By committing to a solution a year in advance, a team forecloses the learning that would produce a better one. The roadmap answers \"what will we build?\" while never asking \"will it work?\" Teams that ship the roadmap faithfully can still ship products nobody wants — they executed the plan and ignored the evidence.\n\nThe second flaw is false precision. Software development involves irreducible uncertainty; attaching dates to uncertain work converts a plan into a promise. Once the roadmap becomes an accountability instrument, behavior distorts: estimates get padded, scope gets cut to hit deadlines, and quality erodes. Or the dates are missed and trust erodes instead. Either way, the roadmap fails socially before it fails technically.\n\nThird, roadmaps are static artifacts in dynamic environments. Markets shift, competitors move, user research confounds expectations. A roadmap drafted in an annual planning cycle and rarely revisited is a snapshot mistaken for a moving picture. When reality diverges from the document, the roadmap is quietly abandoned rather than revised, and with it goes whatever strategic coherence it carried.\n\nFourth, many roadmaps are political documents first and strategic ones second. They serve as negotiation instruments: a slot for the loudest salesperson's client, a line item for the executive with the strongest opinion, a gesture to appease an anxious board. Items accrete without argument about trade-offs. The result is not a strategy expressed as a sequence but a queue of promises — and a queue is not a direction.\n\nUnderlying all of this is a deeper misunderstanding: the belief that the plan is the product of planning. The value of planning lies in the thinking it forces — what we believe, why we believe it, what evidence would change our minds. The document is merely the residue. Organizations that treat the residue as sacred lose the agility the thinking was meant to provide.\n\nRoadmaps that work look different. They are anchored in outcomes — problems to solve, metrics to move — rather than feature lists. They express direction and confidence rather than precision: what we are doing now, what is likely next, what might come later. They are living documents, revised as learning accumulates rather than defended as commitments. And every item traces back to an articulated strategy, so that \"no\" is as visible as \"yes.\"\n\nMost roadmaps fail not because teams plan, but because they plan the wrong things: features instead of outcomes, dates instead of direction, documents instead of decisions. The remedy is not better forecasting but honesty about uncertainty — a roadmap built to change its mind.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
